---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { profile, publicSafetyNote } from "../../data/profile";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const pageTitle = `WhatsApp Commerce at ruparupa | ${profile.name}`;
const pageDescription =
  "Case study about a zero-to-one WhatsApp Commerce pilot at ruparupa involving Gupshup and Meta.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <a class="back-link" href={base}>Back to portfolio</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">ruparupa · Product Manager</p>
      <h1>WhatsApp Commerce at ruparupa</h1>
      <p class="lead">Exploring whether e-commerce could move from a website into a conversation.</p>
      <div class="case-facts">
        <span>Project type: Building a 0-to-1 product</span>
        <span>Stakeholders: Engineering, QA, business, Gupshup, Meta</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        ruparupa is an Indonesian e-commerce platform within Kawan Lama Group's home-retail ecosystem. One of
        the group's retail brands, AZKO, is listed on the Indonesian Stock Exchange (ACES:IJ). With its long
        track record in offline home improvement retail, Kawan Lama Group has a market position in Indonesia
        that is comparable to Target in the US market. Similar to Target building its own D2C platform to expand
        online, Kawan Lama Group built ruparupa as its own D2C platform, allowing customers to order products
        for delivery or pickup at offline stores.
      </p>
      <p>
        One way to expand the channel and retarget churning customers was through WhatsApp Commerce. The
        opportunity was not only to reply to customer messages, but also to let customers choose items, add
        items to the cart, and generate a checkout payment link directly inside WhatsApp. After our CTO signed a
        partnership contract with Gupshup, a WhatsApp BSP partner that helped ruparupa integrate with WhatsApp,
        I was assigned to take accountability for the product from scratch. ruparupa became the first Indonesian
        retail e-commerce adopter of WhatsApp Commerce and Click-to-WhatsApp ads.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The Problem</h2>
      <p>
        One challenge of building an independent platform is that people can feel they already have too many apps
        on their phones and too many accounts across different websites. This can make people uninstall our app
        or stop logging in to the website. In the end, many customers only want to keep and actively use a small
        number of apps they rely on most often.
      </p>
      <p>
        Indonesia has the third-largest WhatsApp user base worldwide, making WhatsApp one of the most important
        and familiar apps on people's phones. This opened a meaningful opportunity for businesses to reach both
        new users and churning users, including ruparupa prospects who might hesitate to download an app or create
        a new account, and churning users who might have already uninstalled the ruparupa app.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>My Role</h2>
      <p>As Product Manager, I coordinated the initiative across several stakeholder groups:</p>
      <ul class="two-column-list">
        <li>Engineering team: Backend, Frontend, and IT Infrastructure</li>
        <li>Quality Assurance (QA) team</li>
        <li>Marketing Customer Engagement team</li>
        <li>Omnichannel team</li>
        <li>Merchandise team</li>
        <li>Data Analytics team</li>
        <li>UI/UX Research team</li>
        <li>Content Copywriting team</li>
        <li>External BSP vendor: Gupshup</li>
        <li>Meta team</li>
      </ul>
      <p>My responsibilities included:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Defining the user flow based on user experience considerations, overseas commerce benchmarks,
          stakeholder expectations, and the capabilities and limitations of ruparupa and WhatsApp Commerce.
        </li>
        <li>Translating requirements into visual wireframes and a written PRD.</li>
        <li>Acting as the intermediary and translator between internal local stakeholders and the international vendor.</li>
        <li>Supporting development, SIT, UAT, delivery status communication, adoption monitoring, and iteration.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section process-section">
      <h2>Working Process</h2>
      <p>
        The working process had two tracks that moved in parallel. One track focused on the product experience:
        benchmarking an overseas WhatsApp Commerce reference, turning the intended journey into a Miro wireframe,
        using that wireframe to align internal stakeholders, and iterating it until the business and technical
        teams could buy in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other track focused on enablement: setting up the WhatsApp number, creating the Gupshup dashboard
        account, and completing the legality paperwork needed before implementation could move forward.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-frame">
        <img src={`${base}images/whatsapp-commerce-process.png`} alt="Working process diagram for WhatsApp Commerce at ruparupa" />
      </figure>
      <p>
        Once those tracks were aligned, the revised wireframe became the shared artifact for discussions with
        Gupshup. From there, I supported implementation across ruparupa's internal technology team and Gupshup,
        helped move the product through SIT and UAT, supported deployment, and monitored adoption and technical
        issues after launch so the marketing team could decide the next action plans.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Cross-Functional Complexity</h2>
      <p>
        The complexity of this project came from the number of groups needed to make one customer journey work.
        The experience depended on business teams defining the channel objective, merchandise and omnichannel
        teams aligning the shopping use case, UI/UX and copywriting shaping the conversation flow, engineering
        and QA making the integration reliable, data analytics monitoring adoption, and Gupshup and Meta
        supporting the external platform layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        My role was to keep those perspectives connected through a shared product flow. The wireframe and PRD
        were not only design and requirement documents; they became alignment tools. They helped translate
        stakeholder expectations into a customer journey, turn that journey into technical requirements, and give
        the vendor enough context to discuss implementation without losing the business intent.
      </p>
      <p>
        This made the PM work less about simply collecting requirements and more about managing boundaries: what
        ruparupa owned, what Gupshup enabled, what Meta's WhatsApp ecosystem allowed, what the internal tech team
        needed to build, and what the business teams needed to monitor after launch.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section outcome-band">
      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <div class="outcome-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>37%</strong>
          <p>
            lower CAC because customers needed less effort to make a purchase: they did not need to download the
            app or register on ruparupa's website through the normal flow.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>15%</strong>
          <p>
            lower average cost per session after WhatsApp Commerce, when we eventually switched our BSP vendor
            for WhatsApp notifications as well because of a better deal.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <figure class="evidence-photo">
      <img
        src={`${base}images/whatsapp-commerce-presentation.png`}
        alt="Presentation slide showing the WhatsApp Commerce launch context and campaign result snapshot"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Presentation photo from the WhatsApp Commerce work, included as a supporting visual artifact for the
        launch context and outcome discussion.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>What I Learned</h2>
      <p>
        This project taught me that launching a new commerce channel is not just about adding another touchpoint.
        The channel has to reduce real customer friction. In this case, the product opportunity came from meeting
        customers inside an app they already used frequently, instead of asking every prospect or churning
        customer to download an app, create an account, and restart the normal web or app journey.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also learned how important visual alignment artifacts are in cross-company product work. The wireframe
        helped turn a vague idea into something business stakeholders, engineering, QA, Gupshup, and Meta could
        discuss concretely. For a complex integration, that shared understanding was as important as the written
        requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        The biggest product lesson was that conversational commerce should not simply copy the website into
        WhatsApp. A strong channel strategy starts by asking which parts of the journey become easier because the
        interface is conversational, familiar, and already present on the customer's phone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section note-section">
      <p>{publicSafetyNote}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="contact-band">
      <h2>Interested in discussing product work?</h2>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="button primary" href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Email me</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={profile.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View LinkedIn</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
